139593" :question/id #uuid "e08ed8c7-c4de-5d20-9dbe-6b75124c6a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-139593" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139593/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139593"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139595/selection/2256e6aa83068e7ec2f14e0db2ed82af8e7e93b59b47f300247ddabbd4aae7f5" :answer/id #uuid "76250024-a931-59bd-91e0-275de55d3783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139595/selection/dd2b93b8730